Westeros reads a client's buffer where it is (no copy), and gives the buffer
back to the client as soon as the client commits its next one. A renderer that
draws later than it is asked to (Mesa's llvmpipe draws when the picture is
swapped) may then read a buffer that the client is already drawing its next
picture into: about one picture in twenty lacked a part (a logo of the user
interface, patches of a video frame) when RDK's window manager drew 36 times
a second. Westeros has the cure for one platform (Broadcom Nexus): finish the
drawing of each surface before going on. WESTEROS_RENDER_FINISH asks for the
same on a platform without fences; WESTEROS_RENDER_NO_FINISH in the
environment turns it off again, for a renderer that needs no such thing.

Upstream-Status: Pending

